655:
An application for a permit to solicit shall be made to the city manager upon forms provided by the city. The application required in this article shall contain the following information, or in lieu thereof, a detailed statement of the reason or reasons why such information cannot be furnished:
(1) The name and address or headquarters of the person applying;
(2) The purpose of the solicitation;
(3) The name and address of the person or persons who will be in direct charge of conducting the solicitation and the names of all promoters connected or to be connected with the proposed solicitations;
(4) An outline of the method or methods to be used in conducting the solicitations;
(5) The time when such solicitations shall be made, giving the preferred dates for the beginning and ending of such solicitation;
(6) The amount of any wages, fees, commissions, expenses or emoluments to be expended or paid to any person in connection with such solicitations, and the names and addresses of all such persons;
(7) A full statement of the character and extent of the charitable work being done by the applicant within the city;
(8) Such other information as may be reasonably necessary and required by the city manager in order for the city manager to determine the kind and character of the proposed solicitation and whether such solicitation is in the interest of, and not inimical to, the public welfare.
(Code 1975, § 10-12)

657:
The city manager shall issue the permit provided for in this article whenever he shall find the following facts to exist:
(1) That all of the statements made in the application are true;
(2) That the applicant has a good character and reputation for honesty and integrity, or if the applicant is not an individual, that every person responsible for such organization is of good character and has a reputation for honesty and integrity;
(3) That the control and supervision of the solicitation will be under responsible and reliable persons;
(4) That the applicant has not engaged in any fraudulent transaction or enterprise;
(5) That the solicitation is promoted solely by a desire to finance the charitable cause described in the application and will not be conducted primarily for private profit.
(Code 1975, § 10-14)

695:
Any establishment in which a billiard room is located is hereby prohibited from having therein or allowing therein or on the premises of such establishment any of the following acts, action, behavior, conduct, goods, or items, to wit:
(1) Gambling or betting of any type whatsoever.
(2) The sale, use, consumption, inhalation, or ingestion of any of the following:
a. Marijuana;
b. Any controlled substance, as defined in Chapter 13 of Title 16, OCGA, listed and contained in Schedules I, II, III, IV, and V as found in OCGA §§ 16-13-25, 16-13-26, 16-13-27, 16-13-28, and 16-13-29;
c. Any dangerous drug as defined in OCGA § 16-13-90;
d. Any imitation controlled substance as defined in OCGA § 16-13-21.
Being under the influence of any drug or other substances referenced in subparagraph (2), above.
(Ord. No. 21/02, § 9, 10-11-2021)
Editor's note— Ord. No. 21/02, § 9, adopted Oct. 11, 2021, repealed the former § 13-140 and enacted a new § 13-140 as set out herein. The former § 13-140 pertained to times when closed to operation and derived from Ord. of 5-12-89, § 6.

706:
(a) No occupation tax shall be levied on the following:
(1) Any practitioners of professions and occupations as defined by section 13-151(g) herein provided;
(2) Those businesses regulated by the Georgia Public Service Commission;
(3) Those electrical service businesses organized under Chapter 3 of Title 46 of the Official Code of Georgia;
(4) Any farm operation for the production from or on the land of agricultural products, but not including any agribusiness;
(5) Nonprofit, agricultural product cooperation marketing associations pursuant to OCGA section 2-10-105;
(6) Motor common carriers pursuant to OCGA section 46-7-15;
(7) Person purchasing guano, meats, meal, flour, bran, cottonseed, or cottonseed meal or hulls in carload lots for distribution among the purchasers for use and not sale pursuant to OCGA § 48-5-355;
(8) Pursuant to OCGA § 48-5-356 for persons selling or introducing into the city agricultural products or livestock, including animal products, raised in this state when the sale and introduction are made by the producer of the product and the sale is made within ninety (90) days of the introduction of the product into the city;
(9) Depository institutions pursuant to OCGA § 48-6-93;
(10) Any business where the levy of such occupation tax is prohibited by the laws of the State of Georgia or the United States; or
(11) Businesses that rent or lease real estate, excluding hotels and motels, consisting of two (2) or less rental units.
(b) The exemptions and limitations contained in this article shall not be construed to repeal or otherwise affect in any way any franchise fees, business taxes or other fees or taxes otherwise allowed by law.
(Ord. No. 98-1, § 1, 6-8-98)

713:
(a) No license issued under this article shall be suspended or revoked except for due cause as defined hereunder, and only after a hearing before the mayor and council of the city upon a prior written notice to the licensee at the address shown on the license of the time, place and purpose of such hearing and a statement of the reason the license would be suspended or revoked. Three (3) days notice prior to the hearing shall be deemed reasonable, but shorter or longer periods of notice shall be authorized as the mayor and council may deem the circumstances justify.
(b) "Due cause" for the suspension or revocation of such license shall consist of the following, to-wit:
(1) A licensee engages in any fraudulent business transaction which is made punishable by the laws of this state;
(2) Violation of any laws, ordinances or resolutions regarding such business or violation of regulations made pursuant to authority granted by the purpose of regulating such business;
(3) A licensee supplies false information to the building official, his/her deputies, any police officer or the city clerk, regardless of when the false information is discovered;
(4) The licensee has failed to obtain or has suffered the expiration, suspension or revocation of any paper or document by any officer, agency, authority or department of the city, state, any other state or the United States under authority of any law, ordinance or resolution of the city, state, any other state or the United States regulating such business;
(5) The licensee violates any provisions of the zoning ordinances of the city; or
(6) The licensee has failed to comply with any provision of this article.
(c) The violation of the provisions of this article by any person may be enjoined by instituting appropriate proceeding for injunction in the courts of competent jurisdiction in this state. Such action may be maintained notwithstanding that other adequate remedies exist at law. Such actions may be instituted in the name of the governing authority of this city or in the name of the city.
(Ord. No. 98-1, § 1, 6-8-98)

721:
(a) It is unlawful for any person to sell, or offer for sale, food or beverages of any type from a food truck without first obtaining from the city a license to operate as a mobile food vendor, except as part of a city-sponsored or sanctioned special event.
(b) An application for a license under this article must be submitted to the city clerk or his or her designee.
(c) The following information must be provided with each application for a mobile food vendor's license:
(1) Name of the mobile food vendor;
(2) Contact information of the mobile food vendor;
(3) Make, model, and license plate number of the food truck;
(4) A color photograph of the food truck;
(5) Copy of an approved permit from the county health department;
(6) List of operating locations and times;
(7) Signatures of the property owner where the food truck will be located during operation indicating the property owner's consent for the use of the property.
(d) Each application must be accompanied by cash or a certified check in the amount of fifty dollars ($50.00) for the application fee. This fee defrays the costs incurred in by the city in reviewing the application. The application fee is nonrefundable. If an application is approved, an additional license fee of one hundred dollars ($100.00) which covers the entire calendar year during which the license is granted. The license will not be granted until the license fee is paid in full.
(e) Review and consideration of an application for a license under this article will be conducted in accordance with principles of due process. Applications may be denied if an applicant fails to demonstrate that he or she meets the conditions and requirements of this article, or where an applicant fails to comply with applicable local, state, or federal law. Any false statement, material omission, or substantially misleading information provided in an application or furnished by an applicant in connection with an application may constitute grounds for denial of an application, refusal to renew a license, suspension of a license, or revocation of a license.
(Ord. No. 20-01, § 1, 1-13-2020)

729:
The following words, terms, or phrases, when used in this article, have the meaning ascribed to them in this section, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:
(1) Amusement game room means any location as provided in OCGA § 16-12-35(b), (c) or (d) where one (1) or more bona fide coin-operated amusement machines are operated that permit non-cash redemption as provided in OCGA § 16-12-35(d)(1)(B), (C), or a combination thereof.
(2) Bona fide coin-operated amusement machine means the same as this term is defined in OCGA § 50-27-70(b)(2)(A) and (B) and any applicable regulations of the State of Georgia. examples of bona fide coin-operated amusement machines include, but are expressly not limited to, the following:
a. Pinball machines;
b. Console machines;
c. Video games;
d. Crane machines;
e. Claw machines;
f. Pusher machines;
g. Bowling machines;
h. Novelty arcade games;
i. Foosball or table soccer machines;
j. Miniature racetrack, football or golf machines;
k. Target or shooting gallery machines;
l. Basketball machines;
m. Shuffleboard machines;
n. Kiddie ride games;
o. Skee-Ball® machines;
p. Air hockey machines;
q. Roll down machines;
r. Trivia machines;
s. Laser games;
t. Simulator games;
u. Virtual reality machines;
v. Maze games;
w. Racing games; and
x. Any other similar amusement machine which can be legally operated in Georgia.
The term "coin-operated amusement machine" does not include the following:
1. Coin-operated billiard table as defined in article VIII, section 13-132 of this chapter;
2. Coin-operated washing machines or dryers;
3. Vending machines which for payment of money dispense products or services;
4. Gas and electric meters;
5. Pay telephones;
6. Pay toilets;
7. Cigarette vending machines;
8. Coin-operated vending machines;
9. Coin-operated scales;
10. Coin-operated gumball machines;
11. Coin-operated television sets which provide cable or network programming;
12. Coin-operated massage beds; and
13. Machines which are not legally permitted to be operated in Georgia.
(3) Location means a business within the city that has complied with the provisions of the ordinances of the city relating to occupation taxes and business licenses and the entire office or area of the business in any one (1) location owned or leased by the same proprietor or proprietors where the lessor or lessors allow the space to be used for business purposes.
(4) Location owner or location operator means an owner or operator of a business where one (1) or more bona fide coin-operated amusement machines are available for commercial use and play by the public or shall have the same definition as found in the OCGA § 50-27-70, should that definition differ.
(Ord. No. 21/02, § 13, 10-11-2021)

731:
Application for a license for operating an amusement game room within the corporate limits of the city must be made to the city clerk upon a form to be supplied by the city clerk for this purpose. The license application must include the following information:
(1) Name, address, and age of the applicant and the date of the application;
(2) Address or place where the bona fide coin-operated amusement machine or machines are to be offered to the public for play and the other business or businesses operated at that place or places;
(3) Name and address of the owner of the machine or machines and a copy of the owner's master license;
(4) Name and address of any other business owned or operated by applicant within the corporate limits of the city; and
(5) List of any other licenses or permits from the city held by the applicant.
Upon issuing a license for an amusement game room, the city official or employee will provide the license with a copy of the ordinance from which this article is derived. A license issued in accordance with this article will be valid until December 31 st of the year in which the license was issued. The issued license shall not be transferred to another owner at the same site within the city. A new owner or proprietor must first obtain a new license if they are going to operate in the same or different location in the city.
(Ord. No. 21/02, § 13, 10-11-2021)

741:
(a) Devices to be kept in plain view; gambling devices prohibited. All bona fide coin-operated amusement machines shall at all times be kept and placed in plain view of and open and accessible to any person(s) who may frequent or be in any place of business where such machines are kept or used. Nothing in this section shall be construed to authorize, permit or license any gambling device of any nature whatsoever.
(b) Inspection. The director of public safety may inspect or cause the inspection of any location in which any such bona fide coin-operated amusement machine(s) are operated or set up for operating, and may inspect, investigate and test such machines as needed.
(c) Attendant required. It shall be unlawful for any location owner or location operator to open the location to the public unless an attendant is present. Said attendant shall be of sufficient mental and physical capacity so as to be able to provide aid to patrons if needed or desired. Said attendant shall not be less than eighteen (18) years of age.
(d) Loitering. As used in this section, "loitering" shall mean remaining idle in essentially one (1) location and shall include the concepts of spending time idly, loafing or walking about aimlessly, and shall be unlawful for any person, firm or corporation licensed to operate an amusement game room to permit loitering on or in the immediate vicinity of any machine or business premises regulated hereunder in such a manner as to:
(1) Create or cause to be created a danger of a breach of the peace;
(2) Create or cause to be created any disturbance of the peace, as defined by law;
(3) Obstruct the free passage of pedestrians or vehicles;
(4) Obstruct, molest, or interfere with any person lawfully in a public place.
(e) Shirt and shoes required. All location owners and location operators shall require shirts and shoes to be worn at all times by any person frequenting their location.
(Ord. No. 21/02, § 13, 10-11-2021)

748:
(a) Every hotelier must, without delay, report violations of law to the city police department that were either witnessed or made known to them by an employer, patron, guest, visitor, or any other person on the premises.
(b) Every hotelier must, at all times, maintain a manager on duty and onsite who is capable of assisting patrons with check-in and check-out services and maintenance issues. In addition, said manager must be capable of communicating, and cooperating with city police or other law enforcement officials in maintaining the public health, safety, and welfare.
(c) All information required to be procured and kept under this article must be kept strictly confidential in accordance with state and federal law and must not be provided to any person except to a federal or state law enforcement officer or to any officer empowered to enforce this article, including city police officers.
(d) All information required to be procured and kept under this article must be provided to any federal or state law enforcement officer or city sworn law enforcement officer empowered to enforce this article, upon demand of the officer and a representation by the officer that a reasonable suspicion exists that such information is relevant to a then-pending inquiry or investigation. Nothing in this requirement may be construed as giving any such officer any greater right or license to enter a room or invade privacy than the officer would otherwise possess as a matter of law.
(e) Unless otherwise allowed under the law and unless presented with a warrant or administrative subpoena, a hotelier has the right to object to providing the demanded information and the right to have pre-compliance review of the demand performed by the judge of the city's municipal court within seven (7) days of the objection. Such pre-compliance review may be conducted in person, in court, via video conferencing, or by other technological means available.
(f) Every hotelier must keep a record of all rental agreements between the hotel or motel and all patrons and their guests and make these records available to the city within a reasonable time upon request. The following information, at a minimum, must be recorded at the time of registration and maintained for a period of no less than one hundred eighty (180) days after the rental agreement's termination:
(1) The full name, phone number, and home address of each patron and overnight guest;
(2) Identification of each patron and overnight guest;
(3) The total number of occupants (patrons and guests) registered in each room;
(4) The room number assigned to each patron and guest;
(5) The day, month, year, and time of arrival of each patron and guest;
(6) The day, month, and year each patron and each guest is scheduled to depart;
(7) Upon departure, a record of the departure day, month, and year for each patron and guest;
(8) The rate charged and the amount collected for rental of the room;
(9) The method of payment for each room;
(10) The make, type, and license number of each patron and overnight guest's vehicle if the vehicle is to be parked or placed upon the hotel or motel's premises; and
(11) Documentation necessary to verify compliance with the time limitation for stays set forth in section 13-202(a).
(g) Annually, the city will provide every hotelier with a list of crimes and ordinance violations that occurred on the property in the previous year.
(h) It is unlawful to procure or provide lodging in any hotel or motel, or any services therefrom, through misrepresentation or production of false identification, or identification which misrepresents the identity of the person procuring or sharing in such lodging or service.
(Ord. No. 22/07, § 1, 5-13-2022; Ord. No. 24/07, § 3, 10-14-2024)

772:
